00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.870
If you think about--

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.600
we talked about molecules.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:27.950
We talked about electrons.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.180
We talked about phonons.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.670
And we talk about the photons.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:36.800
And often, we can
treat them more or less

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.060
parallel because they
are dilute particles.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:44.030
And they have wave
characteristics.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:49.610
And the way that we've
been dealing with this

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.880
is we talked about
energy level, that's

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.380
due to the wave nature and
the quantization of energy,

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:02.090
due to wave nature
of those matters.

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:07.730
And then we see eventually--

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.830
so at a single interface
or model interface,

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:13.730
you have in the first--
you have reflection.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:16.830
And that's mathematically--
for your approximate interface,

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:18.769
that is zero sickness.

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:23.480
You know that is always smaller
than the wavelengths, right?

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:27.720
So this is what
chapter 5 was about.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.680
And from there, you could
calculate transmission.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:35.490
So those were a description
under the wave picture.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:41.480
Then we move on to really the
transition from that wave,

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.250
if the wave--

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.960
the coherence is lost.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.910
I don't need to
consider the phase

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:48.560
information of those waves.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.010
I just need to consider
trajectory of those waves.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:56.030
And that's to say, where
it's completed treating

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:57.020
at the particle, right?

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.300
So if you think about
quantum mechanics,

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:05.030
what Einstein introduced is
the granularity of waves,

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:05.870
of photons.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:09.030
Photons, electromagnetic
waves, are granular waves.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.960
So it's a particle
and wave duality.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:13.700
And the way that
we've been treating it

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:17.610
is, OK, we first try this
either wave propagation

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:19.950
and then eventually,
we completely

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.420
enacted a way we treat
them as a particle.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:25.670
We don't carry the
phase anymore, right?

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:31.490
So there, this is from
the transport perspective.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.850
And then we went to
the Boersma equation.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:39.500
And using the Boersma
equation, we hopefully

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:44.430
have seen all the diffusion
equations or drift-diffusion

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:47.360
equations in bulk materials.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:51.410
And then when you
have boundaries

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:56.600
where the boundary creates some
scattering of those particles,

00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:00.740
just like when you go play pool.

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.810
And then your
particle, your ball,

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:06.617
hits the boundary--
again, reflection, right?

00:06:06.617 --> 00:06:08.450
For most of those
[? transport ?] potential,

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:11.890
there's a diffuse
reflection that

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:17.500
create additional impetus
for the carrier flow.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:24.430
And then chapter 8 was
trying to wrap the idea

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:28.570
that microscopically, when you
think about energy conversion,

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:33.670
it's really the transformation
of one type of energy carrier

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:37.040
energy into another
type of energy.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.320
So that's the interaction, the
scatter and the absorption,

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:43.900
the excitation of
energy carrier.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.930
But at the same time,
you have the loss.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:48.550
You have the transport.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:52.390
So you want to design
a good heat engine.

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:55.670
Your thermodynamics
has to be right.

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:57.970
That scatter-- and we
have energy conservation.

00:06:57.970 --> 00:06:59.660
We have momentum conservation.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:02.770
You can think about that as the
thermodynamics has to be right.

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:05.710
And then your transport
has to be right.

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:10.220
I say, that's the left hand
side of the Boersma equation.

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:13.550
And I warn you that I
couldn't do liquid, right?

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:18.080
So if I think about electron,
phonon, photons, or waves

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.770
periodic boundary
condition and eigenmode--

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:26.300
and so with that, they
dilute particle approximation

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:27.530
as violet.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:31.300
And to deal with--
so the next thing

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.200
is we're going to
talk about liquids.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:39.110
And I will spend
only very little time

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:42.280
on the transport
in bulk liquids.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:45.970
And in fact, I think when
I look at the literature--

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:49.270
I think we don't really
even understand well,

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:52.370
transport in bulk liquids.

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:56.165
And it becomes much harder
because the liquid, as well as

00:07:56.165 --> 00:07:59.070
an amorphous material-- you go
to amorphous silicon, right?

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:02.060
You lose that
periodicity, right?

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:06.080
Crystal is an idea because when
you say, give us structure,

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:11.120
you only have one arrangement,
perfect crystal, purity.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:14.630
But when you say amorphous,
where the atom can be anywhere,

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:17.600
you've got zillions
of arrangement.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.500
So that makes the picture much
[? harder. ?] And the liquid--

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.990
so liquid, in fact,
the difference

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:29.070
between amorphous material
and liquid is very small.

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:33.590
And if you think about
glass, you would typically

00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:36.929
think of this as a solid.

00:08:36.929 --> 00:08:42.480
But the glass, if
you look at people

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.810
starting this say,
glassy face, they

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:49.500
have radiological properties.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:50.940
They could change, all right?

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:58.800
And so first, I gave a
very brief [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:05.790
for the bulk properties, and
how people try to treat it.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:09.450
And then quickly, this
field went to computerized,

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:11.580
computer simulations.

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:16.800
And then I'll discuss
more on the interfaces.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.090
In fact, that's probably most
interesting if you deal with

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:23.910
transport-related liquids--

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:33.060
how the liquid interact
with solid at interface,

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:35.370
including the
electrical bubble layers

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:38.490
and the solid, solid
interaction when we have, say,

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:41.020
these interfaces.

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:43.200
So that's chapter 9.

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.380
And we're talking about
liquids and the interfaces.

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:04.290
And I mentioned, if
you look at the--

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:10.978
for liquid, what are the
historic approaches, people

00:10:10.978 --> 00:10:12.270
trying to deal with the liquid?

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:22.560
One approach is trying to
modify the Boersma equation.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:28.650
Boersma equation is
a dilute particle.

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:33.990
So when you have a more
concentrated, high pressure

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:38.010
gas, for example, the particle
is a lot longer, very dilute.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:39.840
So you have the approach.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:41.510
You modify Boersma equation.

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:51.340
And I'll briefly mention
2-particle distribution--

00:10:57.244 --> 00:11:02.910
distribution function, and the
corresponding Enskog equation.

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:18.700
And then the other approach is
really starting with Einstein,

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:19.940
is the Brownian Motion.

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:31.330
And it eventually develops into
the linear response theory.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:53.430
So those were some
of the directions.

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:58.280
And eventually, this
one didn't pan out.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:00.320
It's not very fruitful,
Boersma equation.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:02.180
They tried to modify.

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:07.400
And so most of
the studies now is

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:12.330
based on computer simulation
and linear response theory.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:19.985
So let's really give a very
brief picture of bulk liquids.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:34.360
And one of the variables of
the function we use often

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:47.680
to characterize liquid is the
radial distribution function,

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:52.860
which actually can be measured
using tools like a neutron

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:53.790
scattering tool.

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:04.480
And the definition
is, OK, I have--

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:10.360
so this is trying to measure,
what's the order of the atoms?

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:13.390
If I take any of the
atom out of the center,

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:19.180
how are the surrounding atoms
distributed around this atom?

00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:25.420
And so the radial
distribution function is 4 pi.

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:32.260
Really, 4 pi ask
r-squared-- the r gives you

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:34.300
the volume around the r.

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:38.950
And this n is the average
density of the atoms.

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:41.510
And this is the
radial distribution.

00:13:41.510 --> 00:13:49.630
So basically, if you look in
this radius r, from the atom,

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:52.600
and look at the
thickness of dr--

00:13:55.900 --> 00:14:02.060
so how many atoms do I have
in this small shell of volume?

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:05.530
So this is the definition
for the radial distribution

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:19.400
function, so the number of
atoms in r and r plus dr.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:24.830
And if you think about the
difference between liquid

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:28.430
and gas and the crystals--

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:30.620
typically the radial
distribution function.

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:37.500
So crystal, if I look at the
atom, this is the center.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:44.080
The next atom is some
distance away, the next layer.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:49.270
And then the next
layer is periodic.

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:50.960
So you continue.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:55.650
That's the crystal
periodic arrangement.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:15:03.120
And so if this is a g as
a function of r and r--

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:07.890
and if you look at
the gas, the gas

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:13.530
is pretty much like a
uniform distribution.

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:19.290
And so there is
really no structure

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:22.480
in the distribution function.

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:24.400
And then you look at the liquid.

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:30.040
Liquid is to say, if I take an
atom, immediately surrounding

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:33.910
there because of the mutual
influence of the atoms,

00:15:33.910 --> 00:15:36.400
there is a little
bit of structure.

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:38.590
And away from it,
there's no correlation

00:15:38.590 --> 00:15:42.220
because the atoms are
moving pretty randomly.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:46.180
So the way I can--

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:50.480
the typical distribution
function of this

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:56.870
is dying off after a
few layers of atoms

00:15:56.870 --> 00:16:00.620
surrounding this vessel, any
of the [? space in the ?] atom.

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:11.630
OK, so what's the use, if you
use the radial distribution

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:12.150
function?

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:15.500
For example, I can
calculate, if I

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:20.640
know the potential
interaction between two atoms.

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:23.990
So if I know the potential
interaction between two atoms,

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:28.040
then I can calculate using the
radial distribution function,

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:32.810
what's the total potential
energy in the system?

00:16:32.810 --> 00:16:38.650
Is it 0 to infinite
integration, 4 pi r-squared?

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:41.690
And this is a
potential interaction

00:16:41.690 --> 00:16:49.410
between two atoms and grdr.

00:16:54.760 --> 00:17:00.070
And if you go back to
the thermodynamics--

00:17:00.070 --> 00:17:05.440
in chapter 4, we consider the
different partition function,

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:08.150
micro-canonical system,
canonical system,

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:10.450
grand canonical system.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:13.660
If you write down the
potential energy and you

00:17:13.660 --> 00:17:14.680
have kinetic energy.

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:15.910
You have potential energy.

00:17:15.910 --> 00:17:20.920
You can actually write down
the distribution functions.

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:22.630
And from the
distribution function,

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:25.089
you can calculate the
corresponding thermodynamic

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:26.010
potential.

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:31.870
A canonical system,
it's the freedom--

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:33.190
free energy there.

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:36.730
And micro-canonical is entropy.

00:17:36.730 --> 00:17:40.450
So once you have this
thermodynamic function,

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:44.290
you can derive all the
thermodynamic properties

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:47.920
of the system, like the
equation of states, right?

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:57.470
So one interesting case
was the equation of states.

00:17:57.470 --> 00:18:01.180
So for example, if you
say Van der Waals--

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:07.540
this is the famous Van
der Waals potential.

00:18:07.540 --> 00:18:11.200
If we take a Van der
Waals potential, phi-r--

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:21.220
the small phi-r, is 4-epsilon
and sigma, or r, 12th power,

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:27.430
minus sigma over r, sixth
power, one rest potential.

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:31.930
And then let's
suppose that I have--

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:36.190
this the radial distribution
function as a step function.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:43.080
the radial distribution
step is zero.

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:45.760
So it's basically-- here,
I'm drawing this way.

00:18:45.760 --> 00:18:50.670
But this step is just here.

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:52.200
This is my d.

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:58.370
So within 0 to d, this
distribution function is 0.

00:18:58.370 --> 00:19:02.600
So this is an r
less than d, right?

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:06.260
You can't have atoms
together and closer than d

00:19:06.260 --> 00:19:10.420
because that's the territory
of the specific atom.

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:15.760
And when r is larger than d--

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:17.320
let's say this is a 1.

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:20.170
So we lost the
structure completely

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:24.640
once this is getting
out of this region.

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:29.380
So if you take these two
functions, you plug it in.

00:19:29.380 --> 00:19:34.660
And what you find out
is that your phi-n

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:37.900
is-- so the total
potential of the system

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:43.070
is an squared over
v, the volume.

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:50.000
And using this potential
energy and kinetic energy,

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:52.940
you go reconstruct the
partition function.

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:56.720
And from partition function,
you get a equation of states.

00:19:56.720 --> 00:19:58.910
And equation of states--

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:14.270
the equation of states that
you have is p equals NkBT.

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:26.180
And wave must be n, minus
a squared over v squared.

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:31.160
And the B is the volume for
the individual molecule--

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:38.370
one molecule.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:48.040
So this is the
equation of states

00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:53.320
that's slightly different
from the ideal gas law.

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:57.770
Ideal gas is p
equals nrt, right?

00:20:57.770 --> 00:21:00.275
And this is Van der
Waals equation of states.

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:13.100
It comes from the Van der Waals
potential and Van der Waals

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:14.270
equation of states.

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:27.160
And it has a very important
historical importance.

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:33.940
And in fact, if you go to check,
probably the Nobel Prize--

00:21:33.940 --> 00:21:39.340
the first one in physics was
in, I think it was the X-ray.

00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:41.440
And the first one in
chemistry probably

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:45.660
was one Van der Waals, OK?

00:21:45.660 --> 00:21:47.400
And why?

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:50.160
Because if you look
at the ideal gas law,

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:56.240
it's p times [INAUDIBLE]
equals nrt, right?

00:21:56.240 --> 00:21:58.730
But the Van der Waals
equation of states

00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:03.610
is actually giving
you the liquid.

00:22:03.610 --> 00:22:06.640
So part of the
equation of states--

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:13.450
so this is for the Van der
Waals gas, it looks like this--

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:20.920
Van der Waals
equation of states.

00:22:20.920 --> 00:22:28.595
And it turns out when you
take a horizontal line,

00:22:28.595 --> 00:22:29.470
you create this area.

00:22:29.470 --> 00:22:31.720
That's where the
horizontal line should be.

00:22:31.720 --> 00:22:34.750
And this is the liquid.

00:22:34.750 --> 00:22:36.940
And this is the gas.

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:40.330
And the in between-- so
for different temperatures,

00:22:40.330 --> 00:22:43.600
you plot it in between.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:48.310
You have the vapor dome, right?

00:22:48.310 --> 00:22:56.200
So you look at the
steam characteristics.

00:22:56.200 --> 00:23:04.200
In this region, you have
mixtures of liquid and steam.

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:09.270
And this is the steam
region, liquid region.

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:13.500
And it turns out, this one--

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:17.880
in between, let's say
this is a, b, c, d.

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:23.010
And the b to c, this
region is impossible.

00:23:23.010 --> 00:23:28.650
And in this region, you don't
have a homogeneous state.

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:30.360
You decompose.

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:31.380
So you've got a mixture.

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:33.810
You've got another one phase.

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:38.260
You get water and steam.

00:23:38.260 --> 00:23:44.580
And this is the sub-cool you can
potentially have, super heat.

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:49.110
And in between,
some of [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:51.420
is absolutely unstable.

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:54.090
So you've got mixtures.

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:55.710
And of course,
normally, we always

00:23:55.710 --> 00:24:02.640
get those mixtures in the
whole from a to d region.

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:08.010
So the importance of the
Van der Waals equation

00:24:08.010 --> 00:24:15.480
really gives you characteristics
from liquid to the vapor phase.

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:18.250
Historically, very important.

00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:21.930
So that's the
thermodynamics part.

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:26.835
And from the kinetics part--

00:24:33.320 --> 00:24:36.010
so that's a transport, right?

00:24:36.010 --> 00:24:41.920
So if you think about
the Boersma equation,

00:24:41.920 --> 00:24:44.920
the assumption we have
in the Boersma equation

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:47.890
is the molecular kills, right?

00:24:47.890 --> 00:24:49.910
The distribution
function at the end

00:24:49.910 --> 00:24:54.520
that we say we can use one
particle in the system,

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:56.770
one particle
distribution function

00:24:56.770 --> 00:25:01.040
to represent the distribution
of all particles.

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:05.510
So if I write down a 2-particle
distribution function.

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:13.000
So I type T. One particle has
a position, a momentum, rp.

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:19.520
And second particle has a
position and momentum, r2p2.

00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:23.260
That's the 2-particle
distribution.

00:25:23.260 --> 00:25:28.540
And the Boersma equation is
based on the molecular chaos

00:25:28.540 --> 00:25:33.200
assumption, which means this
is independent of each other--

00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:38.980
The trp times the tr2p2.

00:25:42.970 --> 00:25:45.740
That's a molecular
kinetic assumption.

00:25:45.740 --> 00:25:47.750
And because of
this, the n particle

00:25:47.750 --> 00:25:52.370
is just a product of
all those distribution

00:25:52.370 --> 00:25:55.040
functions to the n-th power.

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:58.100
And they correct it.

00:25:58.100 --> 00:26:04.280
So at the beginning, there
was effort until '60s, OK?

00:26:04.280 --> 00:26:07.730
People were trying
to do correction

00:26:07.730 --> 00:26:10.940
to this molecular
chaos assumption.

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:14.540
And one of these
is due to Enskog.

00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:24.110
And Enskog is still pretty
much like a hot sphere.

00:26:24.110 --> 00:26:35.000
And TRP and r2 p2 equals ftrp--

00:26:39.260 --> 00:26:45.080
ftr2-- p2.

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:49.530
There is a function-- add
a function to prevent,

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:52.640
for example, the two
molecules or two atoms that

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:55.280
occupy the same location
at the same time.

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:57.810
So you can use that hot sphere.

00:26:57.810 --> 00:27:02.330
So here is the function
that represents the distance

00:27:02.330 --> 00:27:07.680
of the two molecules.

00:27:07.680 --> 00:27:14.160
And with this power
distribution function,

00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:17.670
you'll go back to
the derivation.

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:21.780
You start with the
[INAUDIBLE] equation.

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:25.230
And when you simplify
to 2-particle

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:26.790
and you look at your
scattering term,

00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:34.650
you derive a set of equations
for f, as well as for g.

00:27:34.650 --> 00:27:36.840
What I want to say,
I didn't write.

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:39.060
In fact, I didn't even
write in the book.

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:41.680
It's very complicated equations.

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:43.060
But there were effort--

00:27:43.060 --> 00:27:45.970
and in fact, there
are some solutions

00:27:45.970 --> 00:27:49.060
to the Enskog equation.

00:27:49.060 --> 00:27:53.080
But it was found that
the Enskog equation,

00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:58.330
you can do a little bit
denser fluids, not liquid.

00:27:58.330 --> 00:28:00.310
Liquid is very dens.

00:28:00.310 --> 00:28:02.860
And you can do a denser gas.

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:07.990
But the range cannot
be extended very far.

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:11.410
So because the equation
gets very complicated

00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:16.150
and this route of extending
the Boersma equation

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:18.661
was not successful.

00:28:18.661 --> 00:28:24.350
It was pretty much
abandoned after 1960's.

00:28:24.350 --> 00:28:28.830
So the other direction
came from Einstein.

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:31.800
And Einstein did
a Brownian motion.

00:28:41.660 --> 00:28:44.960
And that was his PhD thesis.

00:28:44.960 --> 00:28:49.940
He wrote this and went
to the Patent Office.

00:28:49.940 --> 00:28:51.440
And they published
all his papers

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:59.000
in 1905, the relativity,
photoelectric effect,

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:02.760
and the Brownian motion.

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:08.000
And so these were by
year publications.

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:15.360
So at the time, he didn't even
there were Brownian experiment.

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:17.330
But he knew a few facts.

00:29:17.330 --> 00:29:20.840
And he knew that if you have--

00:29:20.840 --> 00:29:23.840
so his purpose was
trying to determine

00:29:23.840 --> 00:29:28.580
what's the size
of molecules, OK?

00:29:31.100 --> 00:29:35.420
And he knows that
when you have--

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:41.900
so he was doing more
deduction, thinking experiment.

00:29:41.900 --> 00:29:52.280
Say, OK, if you have particles,
big molecules, you are solvent.

00:29:52.280 --> 00:29:55.780
You put the milk in water.

00:29:55.780 --> 00:30:00.970
And the one thing he knew was
that there is an ideal gas law

00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:08.820
type for those solute
molecules in the solvent.

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:11.470
And this is the
osmotic pressure.

00:30:11.470 --> 00:30:13.770
He knew osmotic pressure.

00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:22.700
And so the osmotic pressure, p,
is the same as ideal gas, nrt.

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:30.730
a is the number density
of those molecules

00:30:30.730 --> 00:30:35.080
in the air, the solute
molecule in the solvent.

00:30:35.080 --> 00:30:37.910
So that's one thing he knew.

00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:42.880
And the second thing he knew
was fluid mechanics, OK?

00:30:42.880 --> 00:30:50.230
And Einstein knew that
when you have a particle,

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:53.050
and in fluid flow, you have--

00:30:53.050 --> 00:30:56.410
of course, this is a
very small particle.

00:30:56.410 --> 00:30:58.840
And the Stokes drag.

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:03.130
When a particle moves in the
liquid, there's drag flow--

00:31:03.130 --> 00:31:05.420
drag effect.

00:31:05.420 --> 00:31:06.635
So the drag force--

00:31:09.470 --> 00:31:12.950
does anybody remember
the drag force?

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:16.130
AUDIENCE: 6 pi mu D.

00:31:16.130 --> 00:31:24.730
PROFESSOR: OK, 6 pi mu
de D. Well, is it D or R?

00:31:24.730 --> 00:31:26.290
OK, I have 3 pi--

00:31:26.290 --> 00:31:27.550
AUDIENCE: It's mu R.

00:31:27.550 --> 00:31:30.080
PROFESSOR: Right, so
it should be R, right?

00:31:30.080 --> 00:31:30.580
OK.

00:31:35.650 --> 00:31:42.400
R times velocity,
Stokes's formula.

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:46.270
And he uses this
to construct the--

00:31:46.270 --> 00:31:48.175
what's the diffusion mass flux?

00:31:53.180 --> 00:31:57.140
So you can do this by saying,
OK, I have osmotic pressure.

00:31:57.140 --> 00:31:59.640
If I have a
concentration gradient,

00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:02.060
I have a pressure gradient.

00:32:02.060 --> 00:32:04.470
So a is the concentration.

00:32:04.470 --> 00:32:09.390
So if, let's say,
in the x direction,

00:32:09.390 --> 00:32:10.910
there's a
concentration gradient.

00:32:10.910 --> 00:32:14.270
You take a control volume.

00:32:14.270 --> 00:32:17.570
And you do your pressure.

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:24.950
So px minus px plus dx.

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:32.330
And I should say, this
pressure at the steady state

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:36.485
[? through ?] the balance of
the drag force on the particle.

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:43.180
Due to the osmotic pressure,
the particles are moving.

00:32:43.180 --> 00:32:47.770
And the resistance
on this particle

00:32:47.770 --> 00:32:51.990
is due to the Stokes
flow, drag force.

00:32:51.990 --> 00:32:55.820
So what you have here is times--

00:32:55.820 --> 00:32:56.630
let's say here.

00:32:56.630 --> 00:32:59.430
This is a pressure
times the cross section.

00:32:59.430 --> 00:33:03.920
Let's say here is your cross
section we're considering--

00:33:03.920 --> 00:33:11.120
and minus 3 pi mu
D. I don't use R.

00:33:11.120 --> 00:33:17.060
And u times the number of
particles in this volume.

00:33:17.060 --> 00:33:23.680
That gives me steady state,
pressure-driven force,

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:30.130
and resistance due to the flow.

00:33:30.130 --> 00:33:34.420
And if you write it
into differential form,

00:33:34.420 --> 00:33:35.380
what you have--

00:33:35.380 --> 00:33:39.610
because AC times, eventually,
dx, gives me volume.

00:33:39.610 --> 00:33:43.250
[? dvA, ?] [? dN ?] and
[? dA ?] gives me another N.

00:33:43.250 --> 00:33:44.680
So I will have--

00:33:54.020 --> 00:34:02.920
what you get out of this is
n times u equals minus one

00:34:02.920 --> 00:34:11.239
third, 3 D mu, dp, dx.

00:34:11.239 --> 00:34:18.409
And dp/dx, when you convert it
into using the osmotic pressure

00:34:18.409 --> 00:34:29.210
relation, we have kBT
and 3 pi D mu and dn/dx.

00:34:29.210 --> 00:34:32.900
So this is the flux.

00:34:32.900 --> 00:34:37.520
If you look at this, number
density times velocity

00:34:37.520 --> 00:34:42.739
gives you the flux
meter squared,

00:34:42.739 --> 00:34:45.969
the number of particles
per meter square.

00:34:49.130 --> 00:34:53.060
So what we have
here, essentially,

00:34:53.060 --> 00:34:57.980
if you think about
Fick's law, this

00:34:57.980 --> 00:35:07.670
is your j flux equals
minus a, dn/dx.

00:35:07.670 --> 00:35:11.350
That's the diffusivity.

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:21.290
And here, the diffusivity
is related to the viscosity

00:35:21.290 --> 00:35:24.500
diameter, right?

00:35:24.500 --> 00:35:32.715
So from here, you can say the
diffusivity is a kBT and a 3 pi

00:35:32.715 --> 00:35:33.215
D mu.

00:35:36.640 --> 00:35:41.190
So from here, Einstein says
you can do an experiment.

00:35:41.190 --> 00:35:43.800
Your drop of milk--

00:35:43.800 --> 00:35:45.060
you measure diffusivity.

00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:49.572
Time it, and you
can visibly see it.

00:35:49.572 --> 00:35:51.470
You know the viscosity.

00:35:51.470 --> 00:35:53.225
This the viscosity
of the liquid.

00:35:55.996 --> 00:35:58.089
You know that.

00:35:58.089 --> 00:35:59.390
You know that.

00:35:59.390 --> 00:36:05.240
You know what's the
diameter of the molecule.

00:36:05.240 --> 00:36:08.020
So it's very simple, right?

00:36:08.020 --> 00:36:13.610
And so that's why this is
called the Einstein relation.

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:17.980
Recall when we talked
about semiconductors,

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:20.230
the semiconductor
has a diffusivity.

00:36:20.230 --> 00:36:23.120
And the mobility has a relation.

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:25.660
And here is
diffusivity viscosity.

00:36:25.660 --> 00:36:28.240
Viscosity is also--
so in the term,

00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:30.430
mobility is, like,
an inverse mobility.

00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:38.240
This is a diffusion
process, right?

00:36:38.240 --> 00:36:42.470
So diffusion is
due to fluctuation.

00:36:42.470 --> 00:36:47.500
Viscosity is a sort of
friction dissipation.

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:53.800
So this is one example of the
more generalized fluctuation

00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:55.360
dissipation theory.

00:36:55.360 --> 00:36:56.980
Here we have fluctuation.

00:36:56.980 --> 00:37:02.270
Here we have dissipation
All right, OK,

00:37:02.270 --> 00:37:04.880
so that was Einstein treatment.

00:37:04.880 --> 00:37:10.770
And in fact, Einstein later
on solved the other way.

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:15.470
So if you do nano fluids, you
go to read the Einstein paper,

00:37:15.470 --> 00:37:20.640
he actually saw, this
is one way to do this.

00:37:20.640 --> 00:37:24.110
And he'd say, OK, you
can also add a nanopart--

00:37:24.110 --> 00:37:27.650
I called it nanoparticle--
those molecules.

00:37:27.650 --> 00:37:30.150
And you measure
viscosity increase.

00:37:30.150 --> 00:37:35.270
So what's the viscosity increase
to the base fluid viscosity?

00:37:35.270 --> 00:37:39.180
And from there, he had another
way to do the same problem.

00:37:39.180 --> 00:37:41.750
So for that problem, he
actually has to solve--

00:37:44.330 --> 00:37:51.080
he actually solved the viscosity
flow problem around particles.

00:37:54.580 --> 00:37:59.830
So you can say he's
a fluid mechanician.

00:37:59.830 --> 00:38:03.430
We didn't go to the other
research, fluid mechanics.

00:38:07.030 --> 00:38:10.810
OK, so that was
the starting point.

00:38:10.810 --> 00:38:15.790
There was a very small book on
the Einstein work in this area,

00:38:15.790 --> 00:38:17.060
on the Brownian motion--

00:38:17.060 --> 00:38:19.930
and very cheap, $10.

00:38:19.930 --> 00:38:23.350
You should go to get
take a look at it.

00:38:23.350 --> 00:38:29.300
And so from here, there
was the generalization.

00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:35.380
So Einstein had said the
lesser generalization

00:38:35.380 --> 00:38:38.755
is due to Langevin equation.

00:38:47.410 --> 00:38:51.160
And the Langevin equation
is a stochastic question.

00:38:51.160 --> 00:38:53.360
Just say, OK, is F=ma?

00:38:56.520 --> 00:39:04.520
But the driving force is due
to random thermal fluctuation.

00:39:04.520 --> 00:39:07.370
So if you write the--

00:39:07.370 --> 00:39:11.540
if you have, say, a particle
in the liquid and surrounding

00:39:11.540 --> 00:39:15.950
fluids, give a kick, random.

00:39:15.950 --> 00:39:21.560
And then this says you have,
then, the acceleration,

00:39:21.560 --> 00:39:24.620
ma, the d velocity, dt.

00:39:27.830 --> 00:39:36.110
And the force acting on the
molecule or atom or particle,

00:39:36.110 --> 00:39:39.450
when you start moving, you
always have a drag force.

00:39:39.450 --> 00:39:42.910
So the drag force
is always there.

00:39:42.910 --> 00:39:45.160
And drag force is
proportional to velocity.

00:39:49.450 --> 00:39:52.780
OK, you can write into
normalized to mass.

00:39:52.780 --> 00:39:55.070
But say it's
proportional to velocity.

00:39:55.070 --> 00:39:56.890
So you can do a vector--

00:39:56.890 --> 00:39:57.580
put a vector.

00:40:01.410 --> 00:40:03.330
So there is always drag force.

00:40:03.330 --> 00:40:08.110
And then you have
random kicking force,

00:40:08.110 --> 00:40:10.180
random force, the fluctuating.

00:40:10.180 --> 00:40:17.740
So that's the random
driving force.

00:40:17.740 --> 00:40:18.790
Here is random.

00:40:23.200 --> 00:40:26.740
And there's some basic relation
because there's a random force.

00:40:26.740 --> 00:40:29.420
So the average is 0--

00:40:29.420 --> 00:40:31.300
itself is 0.

00:40:31.300 --> 00:40:39.580
And this random force
is not related to r.

00:40:39.580 --> 00:40:44.700
So if you do your time
average, they are also 0.

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:48.190
But the driving force
itself, of course,

00:40:48.190 --> 00:40:50.670
is related to temperature.

00:40:50.670 --> 00:40:58.650
So if you say here is
the different time, r--

00:40:58.650 --> 00:41:02.400
at the time, s.

00:41:02.400 --> 00:41:07.230
And the typical
Langevin equation

00:41:07.230 --> 00:41:09.580
assumes this is instantaneous.

00:41:09.580 --> 00:41:11.290
So there is no memory.

00:41:11.290 --> 00:41:22.840
And here, what we have is
2 m eta kBT, and delta, t.

00:41:22.840 --> 00:41:25.150
Delta t is just to
say instantaneous.

00:41:25.150 --> 00:41:28.480
And this random fluctuation
is related to temperature

00:41:28.480 --> 00:41:38.220
and also related to how large
is the dissipation here.

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:40.920
OK, so this is the
Langevin equation.

00:41:43.500 --> 00:41:46.440
And solving this little
Langevin equation-- because this

00:41:46.440 --> 00:41:47.110
is random.

00:41:47.110 --> 00:41:50.640
So actually it could be pretty--

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:52.740
you go through rigorous math.

00:41:52.740 --> 00:41:55.470
There are many different
ways to solve this random.

00:41:55.470 --> 00:41:58.860
You can do a spectral
analysis, for example.

00:41:58.860 --> 00:42:03.750
But in the book, I'm
not going to present

00:42:03.750 --> 00:42:08.970
going through every step, we
have a very non-rigorous way,

00:42:08.970 --> 00:42:17.790
just to say, I multiply both
sides because this one du/dt,

00:42:17.790 --> 00:42:20.700
can be written as d/dt, dr/dt.

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:28.500
So if I multiply both
sides by r and you

00:42:28.500 --> 00:42:33.630
go take the time average,
this term actually drops out.

00:42:33.630 --> 00:42:36.300
So you get a regular
equation you can solve--

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:38.520
an ordinary differential
equation, so you

00:42:38.520 --> 00:42:42.150
can solve for the radius--

00:42:42.150 --> 00:42:46.530
the radius of the
particle from t equals 0.

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:51.250
So here, I'm going to
give you the results.

00:42:51.250 --> 00:42:56.850
So if you do the radius
of this, r square,

00:42:56.850 --> 00:43:00.840
this is where, if you put a
particle here, after some time,

00:43:00.840 --> 00:43:04.130
where it's going to be.

00:43:04.130 --> 00:43:06.640
And this is the random walk.

00:43:06.640 --> 00:43:19.920
And the radius is 6 KBT, m eta,
and then t minus 1 over eta,

00:43:19.920 --> 00:43:24.960
and plus eta
exponential minus eta t.

00:43:29.960 --> 00:43:37.490
So this is the solution for
the distance from the origin

00:43:37.490 --> 00:43:41.550
[? built ?] to the random
walk of this particle.

00:43:41.550 --> 00:43:45.690
And you notice, there
is this long time--

00:43:45.690 --> 00:43:48.450
this term drops out, right?

00:43:48.450 --> 00:43:50.460
So the diffusion, the distance--

00:43:50.460 --> 00:43:55.210
because this is
related to diffusivity.

00:43:55.210 --> 00:43:58.450
So the distance will
be really, diffusivity

00:43:58.450 --> 00:44:01.030
times the time square root.

00:44:01.030 --> 00:44:04.090
That's the order of magnitude
of the diffusion is.

00:44:04.090 --> 00:44:07.870
This is when you saw the
Fourier law of heat conduction,

00:44:07.870 --> 00:44:12.040
you know the diffusion of
heat is diffusivity times

00:44:12.040 --> 00:44:14.320
time, square root.

00:44:14.320 --> 00:44:20.030
So that's the long time
behavior of the Brownian motion.

00:44:20.030 --> 00:44:22.850
So the heat conduction,
all this diffusion,

00:44:22.850 --> 00:44:26.160
is a random walk process.

00:44:26.160 --> 00:44:30.060
And recently, if you
check the literature,

00:44:30.060 --> 00:44:32.550
there are actually a lot
of people still doing

00:44:32.550 --> 00:44:34.470
the Brownian motion experiment.

00:44:34.470 --> 00:44:38.490
And some people say,
we did the experiment.

00:44:38.490 --> 00:44:42.420
We say the initial term also.

00:44:42.420 --> 00:44:48.760
That's the translating at
the start of the kicking.

00:44:48.760 --> 00:44:53.710
So one interesting
comment I have here

00:44:53.710 --> 00:44:58.660
is if you look at the Brownian
motion, look at the velocity

00:44:58.660 --> 00:45:01.960
here, forget about this.

00:45:01.960 --> 00:45:05.740
You see the velocity
will be exponential decay

00:45:05.740 --> 00:45:07.690
because this is the velocity.

00:45:07.690 --> 00:45:09.310
This is velocity.

00:45:09.310 --> 00:45:13.540
If you solve velocity, this
is exponential decay after.

00:45:13.540 --> 00:45:16.360
It's getting quickened, right?

00:45:16.360 --> 00:45:21.010
So all this relation
comes from--

00:45:21.010 --> 00:45:23.940
related to the
exponential decay.

00:45:23.940 --> 00:45:25.250
But it turns out this is wrong.

00:45:28.870 --> 00:45:38.370
And the reason for this is wrong
is in this Langevin equation,

00:45:38.370 --> 00:45:40.620
there is also--

00:45:40.620 --> 00:45:42.720
when your molecules get a kick--

00:45:45.450 --> 00:45:49.260
here you use the
Stokes theorem--

00:45:49.260 --> 00:45:52.080
Stokes drag.

00:45:52.080 --> 00:45:57.680
But when a particle
starts from 0,

00:45:57.680 --> 00:46:00.760
this is a steady state solution.

00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:02.880
And when a particle
starts from 0,

00:46:02.880 --> 00:46:07.230
stationary, gets a kick, you
get a transient solution.

00:46:07.230 --> 00:46:10.620
And that transient term--

00:46:10.620 --> 00:46:16.110
what's significant, if you add
a transient term into here,

00:46:16.110 --> 00:46:22.500
there should be additional
transient term, the Stokes

00:46:22.500 --> 00:46:24.460
force.

00:46:24.460 --> 00:46:26.960
And that transient
term is integral.

00:46:26.960 --> 00:46:30.640
So it depends on
the previous time.

00:46:30.640 --> 00:46:36.040
So this equation actually
becomes an integral equation--

00:46:36.040 --> 00:46:38.750
differential integral equation.

00:46:38.750 --> 00:46:41.470
And if you solve this
differential integral equation,

00:46:41.470 --> 00:46:45.480
you don't get the exponential
decay for velocity.

00:46:45.480 --> 00:46:50.610
You get a power law
for the velocity.

00:46:50.610 --> 00:46:54.170
So if you saw,
velocity is actually,

00:46:54.170 --> 00:46:57.530
if you solve this
transient, is t--

00:46:57.530 --> 00:47:01.760
remember, it's
1.53, three halves.

00:47:01.760 --> 00:47:03.440
So it's a very slow decay.

00:47:03.440 --> 00:47:06.760
Exponential and power are
very different, right?

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:08.290
Exponential is a very fast.

00:47:08.290 --> 00:47:11.270
Power is slow.

00:47:11.270 --> 00:47:21.110
So that's the memory, in fact,
of the Langevin equation.

00:47:21.110 --> 00:47:23.045
And I was interested
in this because--

00:47:26.000 --> 00:47:33.390
so the discovery of this
memory effect was by computer.

00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:39.140
So in 1950 or 1960,
when people started

00:47:39.140 --> 00:47:43.880
doing the computer simulation,
molecular symbolism,

00:47:43.880 --> 00:47:46.925
molecular dynamics,
they found this is not

00:47:46.925 --> 00:47:49.300
the case with velocity,
all the correlations.

00:47:49.300 --> 00:47:52.740
So typically, you calculate.

00:47:52.740 --> 00:47:59.460
Turns out later on, when we we
go to linear response theory,

00:47:59.460 --> 00:48:07.830
you see that
diffusivity is related

00:48:07.830 --> 00:48:09.600
to velocity autocorrelation.

00:48:09.600 --> 00:48:14.220
So it's ut u0, dt.

00:48:14.220 --> 00:48:17.080
So that's a velocity
autocorrelation function,

00:48:17.080 --> 00:48:17.580
right?

00:48:17.580 --> 00:48:21.750
So you can see, if
it's a power decay,

00:48:21.750 --> 00:48:25.560
you would expect that
this is very different.

00:48:25.560 --> 00:48:29.790
Was Einstein's original
relation between diffusivity

00:48:29.790 --> 00:48:34.340
and mobility wrong or not?

00:48:34.340 --> 00:48:38.480
It turns out that even though
he didn't [? include, ?]

00:48:38.480 --> 00:48:41.820
even though he has
an exponential decay,

00:48:41.820 --> 00:48:45.510
but the autocorrelation, at the
end, the result was the same.

00:48:45.510 --> 00:48:47.540
So he was lucky.

00:48:47.540 --> 00:48:50.930
They determined the fluctuation
dissipation relations

00:48:50.930 --> 00:48:51.830
still valid.

00:48:51.830 --> 00:48:55.090
Even the decay function
is very different.

00:48:55.090 --> 00:48:56.600
And this was later proven.

00:48:56.600 --> 00:49:01.250
There was a paper later
proven mathematically

00:49:01.250 --> 00:49:03.920
by some integral
differential equation

00:49:03.920 --> 00:49:05.210
that said this is still valid.

00:49:07.720 --> 00:49:09.970
So I was interested
in this problem

00:49:09.970 --> 00:49:13.810
because for a long
period, people

00:49:13.810 --> 00:49:15.340
are doing this with nanofluids.

00:49:15.340 --> 00:49:18.790
They added nanofluids
in the liquid.

00:49:18.790 --> 00:49:21.250
They say there's higher
cerebral connectivity.

00:49:21.250 --> 00:49:25.510
And their theories say they're
Brownian motion, whether it's

00:49:25.510 --> 00:49:27.130
a Brownian motion, in fact.

00:49:27.130 --> 00:49:30.700
And Brownian motion, if you
do this exponential decay,

00:49:30.700 --> 00:49:32.770
it drops very, very rapidly.

00:49:32.770 --> 00:49:36.310
And you go to do all the
automatic things-- about five,

00:49:36.310 --> 00:49:40.570
six automatic-- too small
to actually contribute

00:49:40.570 --> 00:49:43.020
to heat conduction.

00:49:43.020 --> 00:49:48.210
Then there are other people
at the same time-- we actually

00:49:48.210 --> 00:49:55.010
found the paper that said, oh,
this is a much slower decay.

00:49:55.010 --> 00:49:58.310
And because there's
a power law, and that

00:49:58.310 --> 00:50:03.320
may be explaining the
strong connectivity

00:50:03.320 --> 00:50:05.990
is not exponential.

00:50:05.990 --> 00:50:07.370
And then they went further.

00:50:07.370 --> 00:50:09.560
And they say, OK, it
turns out that people

00:50:09.560 --> 00:50:14.540
prove at least for viscosity
and diffusivity, that's

00:50:14.540 --> 00:50:16.580
a fluid flow problem.

00:50:16.580 --> 00:50:19.080
It's still valid.

00:50:19.080 --> 00:50:23.180
So then the question is for
heat transfer, for energy,

00:50:23.180 --> 00:50:25.220
is that valid or not?

00:50:25.220 --> 00:50:27.588
It's an unanswerable
question, OK?

00:50:27.588 --> 00:50:28.880
Later on, we did an experiment.

00:50:28.880 --> 00:50:31.070
And we don't think
it's a Brownian motion.

00:50:31.070 --> 00:50:34.430
But mathematically, that's
still one thing in my mind,

00:50:34.430 --> 00:50:41.260
is what is the autocorrelation
function for heat

00:50:41.260 --> 00:50:46.060
and how they decay
in a Brownian fluids.

00:50:46.060 --> 00:50:50.500
And when you consider
this time retardation,

00:50:50.500 --> 00:50:53.260
the memory effect, what
people call memory effect

00:50:53.260 --> 00:50:59.360
because it takes time for
the Stokes flow to kick in.

00:50:59.360 --> 00:51:05.690
OK, so that's all I'm going to
talk about for the bulk fluids.

00:51:05.690 --> 00:51:07.580
And I think that
there are actually

00:51:07.580 --> 00:51:11.150
really a lot of very
poor understanding,

00:51:11.150 --> 00:51:14.840
even on the basics, of
transport in liquid.

00:51:14.840 --> 00:51:22.750
And there is some potential to
do good research in this area.

00:51:22.750 --> 00:51:25.360
And next, what are
we moving on is going

00:51:25.360 --> 00:51:30.430
to talk about the interfaces.

00:51:30.430 --> 00:51:34.570
So what's important
is to understand

00:51:34.570 --> 00:51:37.700
the potential interaction.

00:51:37.700 --> 00:51:42.317
And so let's look at the
forces and the potentials.

00:51:50.910 --> 00:51:56.730
And of course, we know force
is the gradient of potential.

00:51:56.730 --> 00:52:00.770
So if I know the potential,
I know the force.

00:52:00.770 --> 00:52:06.840
And what we're looking at is
more longer range potential,

00:52:06.840 --> 00:52:08.880
so due to the charge.

00:52:08.880 --> 00:52:12.410
We're not looking at
the covalent force

00:52:12.410 --> 00:52:18.950
between that typically connects
the atoms to form molecules.

00:52:18.950 --> 00:52:24.140
So when we look at the longer
range potential due to charge,

00:52:24.140 --> 00:52:26.030
you can always start
the most basic.

00:52:36.560 --> 00:52:42.320
So let's do, of course, the
most basic Coulomb force--

00:52:42.320 --> 00:52:43.160
Coulomb interaction.

00:52:43.160 --> 00:52:46.760
We'll have a positive
charge, a negative charge.

00:52:46.760 --> 00:52:49.400
I have this is as
a Q1, this as a Q2,

00:52:49.400 --> 00:53:00.380
and the potential as Q1,
Q2, and 4 pi epsilon r.

00:53:00.380 --> 00:53:05.450
r is the separation
between the two.

00:53:05.450 --> 00:53:07.637
Here is the r.

00:53:11.320 --> 00:53:15.040
And you can use the
basic Coulomb potential

00:53:15.040 --> 00:53:20.710
to construct all other forces
that, due to elect, let's say,

00:53:20.710 --> 00:53:25.450
this charge or region, due
to this Coulomb origin here.

00:53:25.450 --> 00:53:29.050
And so for example,
I want to understand

00:53:29.050 --> 00:53:35.020
what's the potential
between a single charge, Q,

00:53:35.020 --> 00:53:37.200
and the dipole.

00:53:37.200 --> 00:53:39.660
A dipole is a positive
and negative charge

00:53:39.660 --> 00:53:42.420
very close to each other.

00:53:42.420 --> 00:53:47.640
So I'm going to have a dipole
here of positive and negative

00:53:47.640 --> 00:53:49.240
charge.

00:53:49.240 --> 00:53:57.000
And here is minus Q. Here is
a plus Q. And let's say here

00:53:57.000 --> 00:54:02.640
is A. And the distance
of the dipole here is d.

00:54:02.640 --> 00:54:06.450
And the separation--
the dipole-- this d

00:54:06.450 --> 00:54:10.980
is much smaller than the
radius, the separation between q

00:54:10.980 --> 00:54:13.860
and the dipole, that's
r. r is larger than d.

00:54:17.010 --> 00:54:21.630
So you could then just sum up
all those Coulomb potential.

00:54:21.630 --> 00:54:30.360
What's my potential here, is
the potential is equal to Q--

00:54:30.360 --> 00:54:35.130
to this Q or to this Q here.

00:54:35.130 --> 00:54:39.240
And this is a b.

00:54:39.240 --> 00:54:41.520
This is a c.

00:54:41.520 --> 00:54:47.250
So if I write the 4
pi [INAUDIBLE] AB,

00:54:47.250 --> 00:54:52.800
so to the negative charge, plus
this Q to positive charge--

00:54:52.800 --> 00:54:57.810
4 pi epsilon, to AB.

00:54:57.810 --> 00:54:59.880
So I have a AC.

00:54:59.880 --> 00:55:02.840
So you just write
the distance down.

00:55:02.840 --> 00:55:07.550
And do your math, using
the fact that the--

00:55:07.550 --> 00:55:12.410
so, here if I do
the AB, for example,

00:55:12.410 --> 00:55:26.180
is r minus d over 2 cosine theta
squared, plus d sine theta,

00:55:26.180 --> 00:55:28.670
d over 2 squared.

00:55:28.670 --> 00:55:33.840
So that's my-- the
orientation of this dipole.

00:55:33.840 --> 00:55:37.050
This is a theta.

00:55:37.050 --> 00:55:39.080
D-- so it's a 1/2 d.

00:55:39.080 --> 00:55:41.760
That's this distance,
times sine theta.

00:55:41.760 --> 00:55:42.830
That's this one.

00:55:42.830 --> 00:55:46.850
And then minus-- that's
because on this side.

00:55:46.850 --> 00:55:49.850
And that's the distance, AB.

00:55:49.850 --> 00:55:54.950
It's just the hypotenuse
of the right triangle

00:55:54.950 --> 00:55:57.090
when I'm writing down.

00:55:57.090 --> 00:55:59.310
And similarly, you can write AC.

00:55:59.310 --> 00:56:03.890
And then you use the fact
that r is much larger than D.

00:56:03.890 --> 00:56:05.420
You linearize it.

00:56:05.420 --> 00:56:07.670
And what you have here--

00:56:07.670 --> 00:56:16.730
I have minus Q. Theta is a
dipole moment, cosine theta,

00:56:16.730 --> 00:56:22.430
and 4 pi epsilon r squared.

00:56:22.430 --> 00:56:29.510
I'll say beta is the dipole
moment is the Q times D.

00:56:29.510 --> 00:56:36.080
So now, this is in my potential
originally for a single.

00:56:36.080 --> 00:56:39.350
You can say for
two point charge,

00:56:39.350 --> 00:56:43.490
I have the potential
is 1 over r.

00:56:43.490 --> 00:56:47.570
So the force is
1 over r squared.

00:56:47.570 --> 00:56:49.610
Now if I look at
the point charge in,

00:56:49.610 --> 00:56:54.560
dipole the potential
is over r squared.

00:56:54.560 --> 00:56:58.940
And the force is
over r cubic, right?

00:56:58.940 --> 00:57:02.960
Because you want to know
what are those relations?

00:57:02.960 --> 00:57:10.490
And as another example,
I want to find out,

00:57:10.490 --> 00:57:16.100
what's the forced interaction
between two solid surfaces?

00:57:16.100 --> 00:57:17.900
Why I'm interested in that?

00:57:17.900 --> 00:57:23.860
Because I want to say, if
I put a particle in liquid,

00:57:23.860 --> 00:57:28.030
what do they-- what is the
attraction force, whether I can

00:57:28.030 --> 00:57:30.610
keep this fluid stable or not?

00:57:30.610 --> 00:57:33.942
If they glue onto
each other, you

00:57:33.942 --> 00:57:37.740
have, eventually, sedimentation.

00:57:37.740 --> 00:57:40.170
How can I make a
stable [? column? ?]

00:57:40.170 --> 00:57:43.550
You need to know those forces.

00:57:43.550 --> 00:57:50.500
So I have constructed, using
the simple Coulomb potential,

00:57:50.500 --> 00:57:52.630
between--

00:57:52.630 --> 00:57:54.550
a point charge and dipole.

00:57:54.550 --> 00:57:57.480
I can look at the
last what I have,

00:57:57.480 --> 00:57:59.470
when I look at
the dipole-dipole.

00:58:05.950 --> 00:58:11.250
So if you look at two dipoles,
due to the Coulomb interaction,

00:58:11.250 --> 00:58:13.020
because you have--

00:58:13.020 --> 00:58:16.008
the dipole has a
positive or negative.

00:58:16.008 --> 00:58:17.550
Each one has a
positive and negative.

00:58:17.550 --> 00:58:20.220
You interacted
with the other one.

00:58:20.220 --> 00:58:23.470
You got the full term summation.

00:58:23.470 --> 00:58:26.140
And then you add up
all those four terms.

00:58:26.140 --> 00:58:29.620
And you find out that your
potential for dipole dipole

00:58:29.620 --> 00:58:35.910
due to this is r-6, right?

00:58:40.240 --> 00:58:42.970
When we talk about Van
der Waals potential,

00:58:42.970 --> 00:58:46.870
this is the attractive part.

00:58:46.870 --> 00:58:49.810
And then when they
get very close,

00:58:49.810 --> 00:58:51.700
you got the repulsive part.

00:58:51.700 --> 00:58:57.500
Here we're talking about
the attractive half, OK?

00:58:57.500 --> 00:59:02.300
And with this, you can-- so
this is a two isolated dipole.

00:59:02.300 --> 00:59:04.700
Now, with this dipole,
you can go construct.

00:59:04.700 --> 00:59:13.960
If I have a dipole and I have
a solid, what's the force?

00:59:13.960 --> 00:59:19.090
What's the attraction between
this dipole and this solid?

00:59:19.090 --> 00:59:24.520
The solid itself, I'm going
to consider not a solid that's

00:59:24.520 --> 00:59:27.640
made up for--

00:59:27.640 --> 00:59:29.810
not a single point charge solid.

00:59:29.810 --> 00:59:31.780
I say it's neutral.

00:59:31.780 --> 00:59:33.790
Each atom is neutral.

00:59:33.790 --> 00:59:37.990
Then I can approximate that
atom itself as a dipole.

00:59:37.990 --> 00:59:42.520
So really, each point
that I have dipole,

00:59:42.520 --> 00:59:46.600
I'm going to sum up the first
interaction of this dipole

00:59:46.600 --> 00:59:52.370
with all the dipoles
in the solid.

00:59:52.370 --> 00:59:57.970
So if you do that-- so here,
I have the distance, d.

00:59:57.970 --> 01:00:02.380
And let's say, here, I
have my [INAUDIBLE],, the x.

01:00:02.380 --> 01:00:09.340
And because of axis symmetry,
I can consider any x I do--

01:00:09.340 --> 01:00:15.340
I say when I integrate
or, say, infinite.

01:00:15.340 --> 01:00:19.250
If I consider either
any x radial direction,

01:00:19.250 --> 01:00:23.110
so this is like I have a
cylindrical coordinate.

01:00:23.110 --> 01:00:31.240
And I can write the potential
interaction between this dipole

01:00:31.240 --> 01:00:33.970
and the ring here as--

01:00:33.970 --> 01:00:38.710
because it says the
distance is the sixth power.

01:00:38.710 --> 01:00:42.970
So the distance here,
if I look at this point,

01:00:42.970 --> 01:00:47.980
and this is the r,
here I have D plus x.

01:00:47.980 --> 01:00:54.910
So I have a D plus x
squared, plus r squared,

01:00:54.910 --> 01:00:57.680
and sixth power.

01:00:57.680 --> 01:00:59.650
So this is my distance.

01:00:59.650 --> 01:01:05.440
And then I have the lumbar
density of this dipole atoms,

01:01:05.440 --> 01:01:08.230
and 2 pi rdr--

01:01:08.230 --> 01:01:11.930
that's how many I have--

01:01:11.930 --> 01:01:13.280
and then times dx.

01:01:13.280 --> 01:01:15.120
So that's the volume.

01:01:15.120 --> 01:01:19.400
And so dx-- and then
I integrate-- the x

01:01:19.400 --> 01:01:21.720
is 0 to infinite.

01:01:21.720 --> 01:01:24.950
This is my origin,
0 to infinite.

01:01:24.950 --> 01:01:26.750
That's x.

01:01:26.750 --> 01:01:29.900
R, I zero to infinite.

01:01:29.900 --> 01:01:31.520
The order is solid.

01:01:35.930 --> 01:01:38.030
So you do that.

01:01:38.030 --> 01:01:38.810
What do you get?

01:01:38.810 --> 01:01:40.400
You do your integration.

01:01:40.400 --> 01:01:48.860
You get the-- this is a
pi n, and a 6 D cubic.

01:01:48.860 --> 01:01:56.480
So D is the separation between
this dipole and the wall--

01:01:56.480 --> 01:01:57.115
and the solid.

01:02:00.490 --> 01:02:02.840
And that's a potential.

01:02:02.840 --> 01:02:04.730
If I do it once I
have the potential,

01:02:04.730 --> 01:02:05.970
I take the derivative.

01:02:05.970 --> 01:02:11.020
So the force is a D force power.

01:02:11.020 --> 01:02:15.160
OK, now if you go to
check in the book,

01:02:15.160 --> 01:02:17.570
I give example
because people say,

01:02:17.570 --> 01:02:23.365
do I have a slip when I have a
liquid flow on a solid surface?

01:02:26.990 --> 01:02:28.910
So I use this.

01:02:28.910 --> 01:02:31.410
That represents my own opinion.

01:02:31.410 --> 01:02:34.550
I said there is no slip
in the normal condition

01:02:34.550 --> 01:02:37.550
because I'm just saying, when
I have this dipole, what's

01:02:37.550 --> 01:02:39.980
the bonding force?

01:02:39.980 --> 01:02:43.280
And then I look at it and
say, I have the atoms.

01:02:43.280 --> 01:02:48.500
And what's the potential
barrier on the surface?

01:02:48.500 --> 01:02:51.970
And the question is whether
I have enough shear force

01:02:51.970 --> 01:02:56.150
to [INAUDIBLE] those
potential barriers.

01:02:56.150 --> 01:02:59.130
So I guess my point is
that you can take that.

01:02:59.130 --> 01:03:00.830
You can criticize it.

01:03:00.830 --> 01:03:08.030
But the point is, you can
develop your own argument.

01:03:08.030 --> 01:03:12.020
And then you let others
shoot it up and say,

01:03:12.020 --> 01:03:12.880
where is wrong here?

01:03:15.400 --> 01:03:19.990
And of course, if you have very
large shear, you may have it.

01:03:19.990 --> 01:03:22.810
But in a practical
situation, I don't

01:03:22.810 --> 01:03:26.705
think there is a [? slate ?]
between liquid and solid

01:03:26.705 --> 01:03:27.205
surface.

01:03:30.390 --> 01:03:34.410
OK, so this is one
dipole to a all solid.

01:03:34.410 --> 01:03:39.570
And now I can do the same if
I have two pieces of solid

01:03:39.570 --> 01:03:40.665
to a parallel plate.

01:03:51.560 --> 01:03:55.840
So I have two parallel
plates separated by D.

01:03:55.840 --> 01:03:58.600
And if both sides are dipoles--

01:03:58.600 --> 01:04:02.440
and again, now you already
integrated one side.

01:04:02.440 --> 01:04:06.040
You just integrated
this other side also.

01:04:06.040 --> 01:04:09.520
In that case, what
you have is a phi

01:04:09.520 --> 01:04:16.330
equals minus A 12 pi D squared.

01:04:16.330 --> 01:04:22.420
And this A is pi squared
c, and n 1 times n 2.

01:04:22.420 --> 01:04:28.510
The n 1, n 2 are the number
densities on both sides.

01:04:28.510 --> 01:04:31.240
And this A is called
the Hamaker constant.

01:04:41.920 --> 01:04:44.875
How many of you have
heard of Hamaker constant?

01:04:44.875 --> 01:04:46.000
That's where it comes from.

01:04:49.760 --> 01:04:54.030
And so now, this
is the potential.

01:04:54.030 --> 01:04:58.380
So what's the forced interaction
between two parallel plates?

01:04:58.380 --> 01:05:01.620
Van der Waals force,
that's D squared--

01:05:01.620 --> 01:05:03.450
D cubed, right?

01:05:03.450 --> 01:05:09.290
For two atoms, you do D
6, potential D 7 force.

01:05:09.290 --> 01:05:15.230
And for two parallel
surfaces, it's D cubic.

01:05:15.230 --> 01:05:17.390
You can do spheres also--

01:05:17.390 --> 01:05:18.320
different geometry.

01:05:18.320 --> 01:05:21.668
And in fact, you read the books,
people have done all those.

01:05:21.668 --> 01:05:23.960
So with a different surface,
there is a corresponding--

01:05:23.960 --> 01:05:29.550
that integration gives you
different Hamaker constant.

01:05:29.550 --> 01:05:34.280
And by the way, this was
considered-- this a classic.

01:05:34.280 --> 01:05:38.730
There's nothing
really complicated.

01:05:38.730 --> 01:05:39.530
You can do that.

01:05:39.530 --> 01:05:42.670
You go back and you
redo it yourself.

01:05:42.670 --> 01:05:46.330
But that was in--

01:05:46.330 --> 01:05:49.930
I think it was in
1940's or 1950's.

01:05:49.930 --> 01:05:54.340
The same problem was to
put a different spin.

01:05:54.340 --> 01:05:59.640
And this was a suggestion
by Max Born to Casimir--

01:05:59.640 --> 01:06:00.980
Casimir again, right?

01:06:00.980 --> 01:06:05.650
So we said Casimir did this size
effect on thermal conductivity.

01:06:05.650 --> 01:06:10.800
And Max Born suggests
to Kashmir, said,

01:06:10.800 --> 01:06:13.710
this Van der Waals force
can be understood also

01:06:13.710 --> 01:06:16.950
as a zero-point energy.

01:06:16.950 --> 01:06:21.350
So then Casimir went back.

01:06:21.350 --> 01:06:24.580
What he did is, OK, if you
have-- this is the other way

01:06:24.580 --> 01:06:27.270
to look at this force.

01:06:27.270 --> 01:06:31.007
And in this case, there's
no longer electrostatic.

01:06:31.007 --> 01:06:32.090
Here is the electrostatic.

01:06:32.090 --> 01:06:34.980
It's Coulomb force, right?

01:06:34.980 --> 01:06:39.210
But this other way to
understand it is like the mode.

01:06:39.210 --> 01:06:45.630
The energy of electromagnetic
waves stay in this gap, right?

01:06:45.630 --> 01:06:48.360
And so this is the mode.

01:06:48.360 --> 01:06:52.170
And there are many other modes.

01:06:52.170 --> 01:06:56.550
If you think about it, it's the
perfect reflecting surfaces.

01:06:56.550 --> 01:06:59.160
As you move closer
and closer, there

01:06:59.160 --> 01:07:01.740
are more modes
that are excluded.

01:07:01.740 --> 01:07:07.150
So the energy in this
cavity is smaller.

01:07:07.150 --> 01:07:11.230
So if you know the
energy, again, you

01:07:11.230 --> 01:07:13.120
take a derivative
of this energy.

01:07:13.120 --> 01:07:15.280
You get the force.

01:07:15.280 --> 01:07:18.070
And this mode is
zero-point because one half

01:07:18.070 --> 01:07:19.480
hu, that's the energy.

01:07:19.480 --> 01:07:24.950
That's zero-point
energy in the cavity.

01:07:24.950 --> 01:07:28.430
And it's the other
interpretation

01:07:28.430 --> 01:07:30.920
for the Van der Waals force.

01:07:30.920 --> 01:07:35.840
So let's now give you
a quantum picture.

01:07:35.840 --> 01:07:40.570
It's important because now, if
you look at the lots of work

01:07:40.570 --> 01:07:41.515
on Casimir force.

01:07:44.480 --> 01:07:48.385
But this one, of course,
is electrostatic.

01:07:48.385 --> 01:07:49.760
So this picture
is electrostatic.

01:07:49.760 --> 01:07:54.290
So the mode is more getting
into the dynamics, right?

01:07:54.290 --> 01:08:01.340
So later on, there are further
developments by Lifshitz.

01:08:01.340 --> 01:08:03.950
The [INAUDIBLE] Lifshitz,
the Lifshitz book.

01:08:03.950 --> 01:08:06.740
And he developed a
theory based on just

01:08:06.740 --> 01:08:09.890
the electromagnetic wave.

01:08:09.890 --> 01:08:12.890
You really calculate
using dielectric functions

01:08:12.890 --> 01:08:15.110
of median 1, 2, 3.

01:08:15.110 --> 01:08:18.649
So what we have here is
the electrostatic limit.

01:08:18.649 --> 01:08:20.720
But if you do
electrodynamics, you

01:08:20.720 --> 01:08:23.370
can have a generalized theory.

01:08:23.370 --> 01:08:25.560
And that generalized
theory, it turns out,

01:08:25.560 --> 01:08:29.850
depends on the dielectric
constant of the epsilon 2.

01:08:29.850 --> 01:08:33.270
Here, we are talking about
Van der Waals is attractive.

01:08:33.270 --> 01:08:35.790
This tool actually, if
you have another media,

01:08:35.790 --> 01:08:39.210
right media in between, you
can actually have a repulsive.

01:08:39.210 --> 01:08:45.000
The two surfaces can
actually repel each other.

01:08:45.000 --> 01:08:48.920
So some people are trying
to develop that in the MIT

01:08:48.920 --> 01:08:51.319
campus-- a lot of
faculty, several faculty,

01:08:51.319 --> 01:08:56.569
Steve Johnson and
I think Carter--

01:08:56.569 --> 01:09:01.529
[INAUDIBLE] Carter, and several
people are working on this.

01:09:01.529 --> 01:09:04.070
So the idea is, can
you develop a surface

01:09:04.070 --> 01:09:07.399
if there's no friction--

01:09:07.399 --> 01:09:14.060
if you can repel
those self-repelling.

01:09:14.060 --> 01:09:20.840
OK, so with this, we have talked
about the attractive forces,

01:09:20.840 --> 01:09:22.460
right?

01:09:22.460 --> 01:09:26.840
And like I said, if
I want to look at--

01:09:26.840 --> 01:09:33.590
I put a particle in
fluids, and if there

01:09:33.590 --> 01:09:38.439
are only attractive forces,
they will agglomerate

01:09:38.439 --> 01:09:41.670
and sediment, right?

01:09:41.670 --> 01:09:46.410
And you need a repulsive
force to maintain them,

01:09:46.410 --> 01:09:48.149
if you want to have equilibrium.

01:09:48.149 --> 01:09:51.550
You can keep this
a certain distance.

01:09:51.550 --> 01:09:55.180
So in the next lecture,
we're talking about that.

01:09:55.180 --> 01:10:01.380
But I want to go back
to the solar cell.

01:10:01.380 --> 01:10:05.540
I want to show you a few
slides on solar cell.

01:10:05.540 --> 01:10:07.950
I didn't have time last lecture.

01:11:36.800 --> 01:11:44.040
OK, so I didn't show any
of these graphs last time.

01:11:44.040 --> 01:11:46.000
We talk about basic principles.

01:11:46.000 --> 01:11:50.670
So here is Shockley-Queisser
limit calculation

01:11:50.670 --> 01:11:54.120
for the maximum efficiency
of a solar cell.

01:11:54.120 --> 01:11:58.150
And it depends on the
band gap of the material.

01:11:58.150 --> 01:12:01.980
So here, you can see this
is the concentration.

01:12:01.980 --> 01:12:07.740
If there's no active
concentration, you get the--

01:12:07.740 --> 01:12:10.020
this is about 1.4.

01:12:10.020 --> 01:12:11.040
That's the peak.

01:12:11.040 --> 01:12:15.540
And the peak efficiency
is about 23, 20.

01:12:15.540 --> 01:12:18.630
That depends on what kind
of spectrum you take.

01:12:18.630 --> 01:12:22.050
And the original
Shockley is about 28.

01:12:22.050 --> 01:12:26.730
And this is about 31, 32.

01:12:26.730 --> 01:12:28.560
So this was a paper
by [INAUDIBLE]..

01:12:28.560 --> 01:12:32.100
So this is about a
gallium arsenide band gap.

01:12:32.100 --> 01:12:39.670
1.1, this here, that's silicone.

01:12:39.670 --> 01:12:45.510
So silicone and gallium arsenide
are in the good band gap range.

01:12:45.510 --> 01:12:47.650
And here is the efficiency.

01:12:47.650 --> 01:12:51.520
If you concentrate
this 1,000 times,

01:12:51.520 --> 01:12:55.630
by concentration, you
generate more photons.

01:12:55.630 --> 01:13:02.740
If you look at it, then you have
larger open circuit voltage,

01:13:02.740 --> 01:13:06.400
and also higher short
circuit current.

01:13:06.400 --> 01:13:10.870
So you get the
higher efficiency.

01:13:10.870 --> 01:13:13.410
And that's a single junction.

01:13:13.410 --> 01:13:17.400
And here was some historical--

01:13:17.400 --> 01:13:19.680
it's an old one, you can see.

01:13:19.680 --> 01:13:24.150
And the silicone-- the
best crystalline silicon,

01:13:24.150 --> 01:13:26.940
that's this line here.

01:13:26.940 --> 01:13:31.830
And I actually with
the [INAUDIBLE],,

01:13:31.830 --> 01:13:38.250
the group before, they say
they have about 26% efficiency.

01:13:38.250 --> 01:13:41.820
And so actually, this is
a single junction cell.

01:13:41.820 --> 01:13:44.694
Single junction cell is the
best with Shockley-Queisser.

01:13:44.694 --> 01:13:48.480
I'd say about 30, 31%.

01:13:48.480 --> 01:13:52.630
So that's actually
getting pretty close.

01:13:52.630 --> 01:14:00.975
And what's interesting here
is the multi-junction cell.

01:14:00.975 --> 01:14:06.738
The multi-junction cell now
is about 41, 42% efficiency--

01:14:10.590 --> 01:14:11.860
43?

01:14:11.860 --> 01:14:12.360
Yeah.

01:14:12.360 --> 01:14:13.705
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] 43.

01:14:13.705 --> 01:14:14.330
PROFESSOR: Huh?

01:14:14.330 --> 01:14:15.060
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] 43.

01:14:15.060 --> 01:14:15.690
PROFESSOR: OK.

01:14:15.690 --> 01:14:19.890
43, they split--
that's by wavelength,

01:14:19.890 --> 01:14:22.080
split into different cells.

01:14:22.080 --> 01:14:23.530
That's another one.

01:14:23.530 --> 01:14:24.390
AUDIENCE: One.

01:14:24.390 --> 01:14:25.420
PROFESSOR: It's one?

01:14:25.420 --> 01:14:25.920
OK.

01:14:29.280 --> 01:14:38.540
But see, I think here, this
one is the cad telluride.

01:14:38.540 --> 01:14:41.070
That's the first solar.

01:14:41.070 --> 01:14:42.760
It's a lower efficiency.

01:14:42.760 --> 01:14:45.330
It's a direct gap solar cell.

01:14:45.330 --> 01:14:50.220
And the first solar, cadmium
telluride, you almost

01:14:50.220 --> 01:14:51.990
get the worst in the world.

01:14:51.990 --> 01:14:55.470
But they are most
profitable company.

01:14:55.470 --> 01:14:58.710
Because cadmium is a poison.

01:14:58.710 --> 01:15:00.690
It's toxic, environmentally.

01:15:00.690 --> 01:15:03.540
And you go to Japan, they
had an environmental disaster

01:15:03.540 --> 01:15:04.200
in the 60s.

01:15:04.200 --> 01:15:07.800
They never touch cadmium.

01:15:07.800 --> 01:15:12.150
But first solar,
they use a thin film.

01:15:12.150 --> 01:15:15.990
Their film is a few microns
because the optical absorption

01:15:15.990 --> 01:15:17.190
is a good direct gap.

01:15:17.190 --> 01:15:22.200
And they are selling about 11%--

01:15:22.200 --> 01:15:25.140
10% to 11% efficiency.

01:15:25.140 --> 01:15:27.810
The silicon cell is
[INAUDIBLE] anywhere.

01:15:27.810 --> 01:15:29.080
This is lab efficient.

01:15:29.080 --> 01:15:30.270
That's the best efficiency.

01:15:30.270 --> 01:15:33.660
Silicon cell is selling
anywhere between 15% and 18%

01:15:33.660 --> 01:15:36.110
or 20% efficiency.

01:15:36.110 --> 01:15:38.920
So at the end, there
is a cost because they

01:15:38.920 --> 01:15:41.890
are the most profitable
because the silicon is about--

01:15:41.890 --> 01:15:43.530
a few years ago, $3.

01:15:43.530 --> 01:15:49.980
So now it's-- this cost
curve is dropping very fast.

01:15:49.980 --> 01:15:59.400
And a lot of work is done
on the polymer solar cell.

01:15:59.400 --> 01:16:02.160
And you see the history there.

01:16:02.160 --> 01:16:06.960
And this should be the diesel.

01:16:09.700 --> 01:16:11.450
It's an open circle.

01:16:11.450 --> 01:16:13.370
That's the diesel.

01:16:13.370 --> 01:16:21.380
And that's what-- it should
be around 11% [INAUDIBLE]..

01:16:21.380 --> 01:16:26.480
But see, eventually,
which one is going to win?

01:16:26.480 --> 01:16:29.180
Which one goes first
to get the grid parity?

01:16:29.180 --> 01:16:35.750
Grid parity means it can compete
with coals and natural gas.

01:16:35.750 --> 01:16:39.350
It's very cost sensitive.

01:16:39.350 --> 01:16:43.700
Multi-junction cells is trying
to use different spectrum

01:16:43.700 --> 01:16:45.110
of the wavelengths.

01:16:45.110 --> 01:16:47.930
And it's actually
really quite ingenious,

01:16:47.930 --> 01:16:51.950
in the sense you have a wider
gap semiconductor on top.

01:16:51.950 --> 01:16:54.950
So the red light
doesn't get absorbed,

01:16:54.950 --> 01:16:57.770
go through [INAUDIBLE]
on the bottom.

01:16:57.770 --> 01:17:00.500
But you have to
connect them correctly.

01:17:00.500 --> 01:17:03.230
Each cell has to generate--
because you're not putting

01:17:03.230 --> 01:17:04.940
the cell in series, right?

01:17:04.940 --> 01:17:07.160
The top cell generates
a certain current

01:17:07.160 --> 01:17:08.330
that goes to the next cell.

01:17:08.330 --> 01:17:11.070
So the current has to match.

01:17:11.070 --> 01:17:14.030
And also, typically
when people connect,

01:17:14.030 --> 01:17:17.420
they do quantum
tunneling-- tunnel junction

01:17:17.420 --> 01:17:19.600
to connect them.

01:17:19.600 --> 01:17:24.030
So if you make an
infinite junction,

01:17:24.030 --> 01:17:27.120
practically, people
[INAUDIBLE] junctions

01:17:27.120 --> 01:17:29.490
because when you grow
different materials,

01:17:29.490 --> 01:17:30.870
they become very crabby.

01:17:30.870 --> 01:17:33.810
And once mature becomes
crabby, it doesn't work.

01:17:33.810 --> 01:17:37.020
Recombination is too fast.

01:17:37.020 --> 01:17:41.100
But theoretically, if
you do infinite junction,

01:17:41.100 --> 01:17:43.710
you can get around
86% efficiency.

01:17:47.880 --> 01:17:50.980
And recombination,
we already mentioned.

01:17:50.980 --> 01:17:57.480
So this is where the silicon
is very good, abundant.

01:17:57.480 --> 01:18:00.690
This optimal absorption,
that's really not good.

01:18:00.690 --> 01:18:01.830
That's the problem.

01:18:01.830 --> 01:18:03.050
See, [INAUDIBLE] the cost.

01:18:03.050 --> 01:18:06.690
So you need the thicker one
to get the absorption up.

01:18:06.690 --> 01:18:09.420
And that's related to
materials because you have

01:18:09.420 --> 01:18:11.040
to use very pure materials.

01:18:14.730 --> 01:18:18.170
So this is the single
crystalline and

01:18:18.170 --> 01:18:18.920
polycrystalline.

01:18:18.920 --> 01:18:23.060
If you go out, you look
at this, that's your--

01:18:23.060 --> 01:18:25.490
you pretend you're an
expert, whether you

01:18:25.490 --> 01:18:27.380
have a polycrystalline.

01:18:27.380 --> 01:18:30.015
Look at this-- colorful.

01:18:30.015 --> 01:18:30.515
That's poly.

01:18:33.440 --> 01:18:35.596
That looks like a
more monocrystalline.

01:18:40.640 --> 01:18:44.270
And then people are
trying to do thin film,

01:18:44.270 --> 01:18:47.540
for example, amorphous silicone.

01:18:47.540 --> 01:18:51.090
With amorphous silicone,
the problem is the mobility.

01:18:51.090 --> 01:18:53.590
Charge does not
travel very well.

01:18:53.590 --> 01:18:58.470
So even though you can use a
500 nanometer amorphous silicon,

01:18:58.470 --> 01:19:00.675
it's still too thick
for electron holes.

01:19:03.900 --> 01:19:06.270
So you want to make
it even thinner.

01:19:06.270 --> 01:19:09.510
And once you get a very
thin, the optical absorption

01:19:09.510 --> 01:19:10.878
is also a problem.

01:19:10.878 --> 01:19:12.795
And here, people have
actually tried to make--

01:19:15.660 --> 01:19:16.710
you can see here.

01:19:16.710 --> 01:19:18.180
You have amorphous silicon.

01:19:18.180 --> 01:19:19.890
That's the widest band gap.

01:19:19.890 --> 01:19:23.010
And then you have
microcrystalline silicon,

01:19:23.010 --> 01:19:26.340
or you can have silicon
germanium with a different band

01:19:26.340 --> 01:19:31.130
gap, so that you can
multi-junction cells.

01:19:31.130 --> 01:19:33.320
This is a polymer.

01:19:33.320 --> 01:19:37.890
Polymer, unlike the
typical pin junction,

01:19:37.890 --> 01:19:40.750
where you got a potential--

01:19:40.750 --> 01:19:43.530
in fact, the potential
is not that important.

01:19:43.530 --> 01:19:48.400
Just by diffusion you find
the concentration gradient.

01:19:48.400 --> 01:19:55.600
And the polymer problem
is the molecular chain.

01:19:55.600 --> 01:19:57.970
You excite the electron hole.

01:19:57.970 --> 01:20:00.250
The problem is that
the electron hole,

01:20:00.250 --> 01:20:01.700
they're bound to each other.

01:20:01.700 --> 01:20:03.280
So they actually
have a lower energy.

01:20:03.280 --> 01:20:05.200
That's acetone.

01:20:05.200 --> 01:20:10.120
And what you want is
to extract them out.

01:20:10.120 --> 01:20:13.180
The electron goes one side,
hole goes to the other side.

01:20:13.180 --> 01:20:16.240
But acetone, if they bind
to each other, eventually

01:20:16.240 --> 01:20:16.820
they die.

01:20:16.820 --> 01:20:18.310
They release heat.

01:20:18.310 --> 01:20:19.480
You're doomed, right?

01:20:19.480 --> 01:20:23.790
So first you need to
separate the acetone.

01:20:23.790 --> 01:20:26.880
And then get it
onto the polymer.

01:20:26.880 --> 01:20:29.080
Again, the conductivity
is not good.

01:20:29.080 --> 01:20:32.400
So people are doing this
three dimensionally.

01:20:32.400 --> 01:20:33.992
One is a hole conductor.

01:20:33.992 --> 01:20:35.700
The other is an electron
conductor that's

01:20:35.700 --> 01:20:37.900
bulk junction solar cells.

01:20:37.900 --> 01:20:39.960
Even though when I
say bulk junction,

01:20:39.960 --> 01:20:43.380
it means that it's a
penetrating junctions, PM,

01:20:43.380 --> 01:20:44.400
[INAUDIBLE] materials.

01:20:47.230 --> 01:20:52.030
And also, you have
to make it very thin.

01:20:52.030 --> 01:20:55.210
And Gratzel cell,
electrochemical cell--

01:20:59.350 --> 01:21:07.780
they use, really, molecules,
their dye, as the synthesizer.

01:21:07.780 --> 01:21:09.820
And it's a monolayer.

01:21:09.820 --> 01:21:13.000
So they coat them, every
nanoparticle, with monolayer,

01:21:13.000 --> 01:21:15.100
to increase absorption.

01:21:15.100 --> 01:21:18.310
Again, you get the
electron hole, the acetone.

01:21:18.310 --> 01:21:23.500
And once you get that,
because it's a monolayer,

01:21:23.500 --> 01:21:25.210
so you generate
the electron hole.

01:21:25.210 --> 01:21:27.580
And one goes to the solid.

01:21:27.580 --> 01:21:31.080
That's the titanium oxide.

01:21:31.080 --> 01:21:33.120
Titanium oxide is in cosmetics.

01:21:33.120 --> 01:21:35.220
So it's pretty cheap.

01:21:35.220 --> 01:21:38.140
And the hole goes to the liquid.

01:21:38.140 --> 01:21:41.430
So the liquid
oxidizes and diffuses

01:21:41.430 --> 01:21:44.250
through the electrode--

01:21:44.250 --> 01:21:45.300
and then reduced.

01:21:45.300 --> 01:21:49.170
And so this is where,
because it's a liquid,

01:21:49.170 --> 01:21:51.420
it's not very reliable.

01:21:51.420 --> 01:21:56.020
And the reliability
is a big problem.

01:21:56.020 --> 01:21:57.720
And so people say
this could be cheap.

01:21:57.720 --> 01:22:02.180
But so far, nobody has been
able to commercialize them.

01:22:02.180 --> 01:22:04.820
OK, so that's what
people are trying to do.

01:22:04.820 --> 01:22:12.080
OK, so here is a first gen,
silicon-based, thin film-based,

01:22:12.080 --> 01:22:15.890
whatever solar you can try
to get a lower efficiency--

01:22:15.890 --> 01:22:18.080
higher efficient, low cost.

01:22:18.080 --> 01:22:20.600
There's a lot of
people working on this.

01:22:20.600 --> 01:22:24.580
OK, stop here.

01:22:24.580 --> 01:22:27.750
And just a reminder,
I'll send you an email.

01:22:27.750 --> 01:22:31.550
Now you can vent all your angry.

